  "interpretation": [
    "Public parse ratio of medians is8.19% lower, with13/15 pairs faster. This observed difference is not the isolated causal benefit of fixed field arrays.",
    "Unchanged typed parse is8.56% lower by ratio of medians, with11/15 faster pairs. Its allocation counters are exactly unchanged. Similar movement on this negative control prevents attributing the full public-parse shift to allocation removal. Do not subtract the two median percentages to estimate an adjusted effect.",
    "All stages use single-operation count1 batches. Native parse durations near60-70 microseconds are vulnerable to scheduling, cache state and process communication cadence, even though elapsed time excludes transport. Raw long-tail observations remain included.",
    "The typed parse14th trial has JS4.46775ms/control1.02725ms/candidate0.775416ms, substantially above respective medians. Public-parse ranges are much tighter: control0.180500-0.252083ms and candidate0.173166-0.230000ms. The severe typed-parse outlier is evidence of instability, not proof of a particular OS mechanism or a shared outlier in every stage.",
    "One-second warmups and cyclic/reversed15-trial ordering limit simple position bias but do not remove shared-host or serial dependence. Host snapshots show mobileassetd/WindowServer/other apps; no compiler bypass is alleged.",
    "Other negative controls move approximately-1.2% source,+1.2% typed retained,+1.17% mutable. No demonstrated general runtime benefit. Consult paired intervals and ranges instead of declaring every control shift random.",
